\f4\fs26 Finding the body of Emil took Detective Supt Lanning next to no effort at all. She figured he might be dead and issued all hospitals, morgues and funeral homes with a \'93Search and Comply Order\'94, immediately getting a response from an enthusiastic and giggly pathologist. By the end of the day she had a copy of the preliminary report, along with the all gruesome holographs and vids, which unequivocally found the cause of the death to be testicular cancer and related metastases. The body was yet to be transferred and she took the opportunity to make a visit to the morgue presently holding him on ice. \
Pungent fumes of hospital strength disinfectant promptly mugged her senses and triggered a stream of salt water from the corner of each eye. She did not know why, but she expected all morgue attendants and pathologists to be pale skinned ghouls with black lipstick and an even darker sense of humour, so it was with a little surprise when she was met by a pretty young blonde with pig-tails, a deeply tanned complexion and a wide bright smile. \
\'93Dr Katherine Logan MBBS PhD L.5, but please call me Kathy. I am so happy to meet you Superintendent!\'94 Kathy gushed through the ever broadening grin.\
\'93Hayley Lanning, PhD, L.5. I want to see all records relating to Emil Kalheri. I was notified by this office that he is here at the moment.\'94 Announced Detective Supt Lanning in her most official voice, hoping the tone might make the happy face disappear. \
\'93Emil Kalheri? Yes, he\'92s a guest at the moment. DNA identification, definitely him. Checked in\'85..yep, yesterday.\'94 The bubbly smile said as she checked the data files. \'93He\'92s not due for checkout until\'85.Ooh that\'92s interesting.\'94\
\'93What\'92s that?\'94\
\'93The body can be released immediately. The fee is already paid. Wow!\'94 She was wide-eyed and seemed gob-smacked by this little revelation, but still very cheerful. \'93Very unusual.\'94\
 \
\'93Who paid?\'94 Detective Lanning was interested. Whoever holds the purse strings, runs the show.\
\'93That is the interesting bit, I can\'92t get that information up.\'94 Dr Logan laughed. \'93What\'92s all the curiosity for this man anyway?\'94 She probed, \'93The spooks were here yesterday asking all sorts of questions about him.\'94\
\'93Spooks?\'94 Supt Lanning\'92s attention level was immediately raised. \'93Do you mean Covert Company Contractors?\'94\
\'93I don\'92t know what they are called. He was creepy, slimy and scary as all hell.\'94 She recalled with a small shudder. \'93He was not fooling around, I\'92m telling you. He made sure I saw his gun, too. He asked loads of questions, searched the body and his property and then left.\'94\
\'93What did he want?\'94 Hayley softly pressed the pretty ghoul.\
\'93He asked a lot of questions, but he was mainly interested in the property. Was there any other property? Where is the rest of his stuff? We told him he had access to all the artifacts the body arrived with, but he was not happy with that.\'94 \
\'93Do you think you would recognise him?\'94\
\'93Yes, I think so, but I am better with dead bodies.\'94 She laughed out loud. Hayley figured that was what passed for humour in pathology circles.\
\'93Can I examine the body?\'94 She never looked forward to checking out the deceased, but it was necessary and quite often productive.\
\'93Of course. He\'92s in drawer 213, this way.\'94 Dr. Cheerful skipped down a long corridor, past the main necropsy room to central storage where hundreds of the recently unlucky lay, mutely awaiting their final ride home. \'93Here he is, the man of the hour, Emil Kalheri. Ta Da.\'94 Kathy clapped and laughed as she stepped back, allowing the now very annoyed Police Detective access.\
She took her time and examined the body carefully, methodically, forensically. His hard worked hands were huge and layered in a miscellany of callouses, cuts and grazes and adorned with disfigured bent digits, many times broken and re-broken. His body swore silent testament to a life of burdensome physical labour and inaccessible health care, with lumpy poorly knitted fractures and jagged scars punctuating a ruddy malnourished frame. She identified only one minor blemish that appeared to be recent, a small circular red mark, no larger than a thumb nail on the inside of the right thigh.\
\'93What do you think this is?\'94 Hayley asked, pointing to the small perfect circle.\
\'93My best guess would be a mild burn of some sort. It looks pretty recent, but it\'92s a bit hard to tell. I\'92ll get a sample.\'94 Kathy burst into action and within a minute she had sliced a decent chunk out of the Emil\'92s dead leg and sent it for testing. \'93We should get that back tomorrow, unless it is urgent, but you know how much we charge for rush jobs, don\'92t you?\'94\
\'93It\'92s a rush job.\'94 Lanning\
\'93What about the cost?\'94 Kathy did not want to get stuck with an unpaid invoice.\
\'93Bill the anonymous benefactors. If you get a complaint, we will know where the money is coming from.\'94 Lanning. \'93Now, the report says the cause of death was testicular cancer. Is that correct?\'94 \
\'93Yes, it had spread to his bones and he was terminal, but he also had a massive dose of AMT in his blood, enough to keep an elephant awake for a month\'94 Kathy explained.\
\'93AMT? Where would a labourer get hold of AMT?\'94\
\'93It\'92s not available at all and as far as I know has not been manufactured in years. I could not even hazard a guess as to where he got it.\'94 Kathy was most sincere, but still cheery and permanently exposing her teeth.\

